      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2011 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Studies have demonstrated that oxytocin (OXT) plays important roles in pain modulation in the central nervous system, and there are OXT receptors in the periaqueductal grey (PAG). The experiment was designed to investigate the effect of OXT in the PAG on antinociception. The results showed that (1) intra-PAG injection of OXT increased the pain threshold, whereas the local administration of the high specific OXT receptor antagonist, desGly-NH(2), d(CH(2))(5)[D-Tyr(2), Thr-sup-4]OVT decreased the pain threshold in a dose-dependent manner; (2) Pain stimulation could elevate OXT concentration in the PAG perfusion liquid. The data suggested that OXT in the PAG was involved in the antinociceptive process through the OXT receptor."
      },
